@anon.814f0bdf02fb37ec5ace91213a028c11.893 = private unnamed_addr constant [1456 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for constant imports that are aliased to non-constant-style\0Anames.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A[PEP 8] recommends naming conventions for classes, functions,\0Aconstants, and more. The use of inconsistent naming styles between\0Aimport and alias names may lead readers to expect an import to be of\0Aanother type (e.g., confuse a Python class with a constant).\0A\0AImport aliases should thus follow the same naming style as the member\0Abeing imported.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom example import CONSTANT_VALUE as ConstantValue\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom example import CONSTANT_VALUE\0A```\0A\0A## Note\0AIdentifiers consisting of a single uppercase character are ambiguous under\0Athe rules of [PEP 8], which specifies `CamelCase` for classes and\0A`ALL_CAPS_SNAKE_CASE` for constants. Without a second character, it is not\0Apossible to reliably guess whether the identifier is intended to be part\0Aof a `CamelCase` string for a class or an `ALL_CAPS_SNAKE_CASE` string for\0Aa constant, since both conventions will produce the same output when given\0Aa single input character. Therefore, this lint rule does not apply to cases\0Awhere the imported identifier consists of a single uppercase character.\0A\0AA common example of a single uppercase character being used for a class\0Aname can be found in Django's `django.db.models.Q` class.\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.pep8-naming.ignore-names`\0A- `lint.pep8-naming.extend-ignore-names`\0A\0A[PEP 8]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/\0A", align 1
